WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.080
Long teams often find it exciting to surpass human level performance on a specific,

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.080
recognition classification task.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:12.080
Let's talk over some of the things you see if you're trying to accomplish this yourself.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:18.080
We've discussed before how machine learning progress gets harder as you approach or even surpass

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.080
human level performance.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:23.080
Let's talk over one more example of why that's the case.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:28.080
Let's say you have a problem where a team of humans discussing and debating

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:38.080
achieves 0.5% error, a single human, 1% error, and you have an algorithm with 0.6% training error and 0.8% depth error.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:46.080
So in this case, what is the avoidable bias?

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.080
So this one is relatively easier to answer.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.080
0.5% is your estimate of Bayes error.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:57.080
So your avoidable bias, you're not going to use this 1% number as reference.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.080
You can use this difference.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:06.080
So maybe you estimate your avoidable bias as at least 0.1% and your variance as 0.2%.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:14.080
So there's maybe more to do to reduce your variance than your avoidable bias, perhaps.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.080
But now let's take a harder example.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.080
Let's say a team of humans and a single human performance, the same as before.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:28.080
But your algorithm gets 0.3% training error and 0.4% depth error.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.080
Now what is the avoidable bias?

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.080
It's now actually much harder to answer that.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:41.080
Is the fact that your training error 0.3% does this mean you've overfitted by 0.2%?

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:49.080
Or is Bayes error actually 0.1% or maybe is Bayes error 0.2% or maybe Bayes error is 0.3%?

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.080
You don't really know.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:02:05.080
But Bayes on the information given in this example is actually, you actually don't have enough information to tell if you should focus on reducing bias or reducing variance in your algorithm.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.080
So that slows down the efficiency of which you should make progress.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:21.080
Moreover, if your error is already better than even a team of humans looking at and then discussing and debating the right label for an example,

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:30.080
then it's just also harder to rely on human intuition to tell your algorithm what are ways that your algorithm could still improve the performance.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:36.080
So in this example, once you've surpassed this 0.5% threshold,

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:43.080
your options or ways of making progress on the machine learning problem are just less clear.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.080
It doesn't mean you can't make progress.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:48.080
You can always, you might still make significant progress,

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:55.080
but some of the tools you have for pointing you in a clear direction just don't work as well.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:02.080
Now, there aren't many problems where machine learning significantly surpasses human level performance.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:08.080
For example, I think online advertising, estimating how likely someone has to click on that,

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.080
probably learning algorithms do that much better today than any human could,

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:17.080
or making product recommendations, recommending movies or books to you.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:23.080
I think that websites today can do that much better than maybe even your closest friends can.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:27.080
Or logistics, predicting how long it will take you to drive from A to B,

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.080
or predicting how long it will take a delivery vehicle to drive from A to B,

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:39.080
or trying to predict whether someone will repay a loan and therefore whether or not you should approve a loan offer.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:46.080
All of these are problems where I think today machine learning faster surpasses a single human's performance.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.080
Notice something about these four examples.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:54.080
All four of these examples are actually learning from structured data,

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.080
where you might have a database of what ad users have clicked on,

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.080
database of products you've bought before,

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.080
a database of how long it takes to get from A to B,

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:07.080
database of previous loan applications and their outcomes.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:11.080
And these are not natural perception problems.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:18.080
So these are not computer vision or speech recognition or natural language processing tasks.

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:23.080
Humans tend to be very good at natural perception tasks.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:31.080
So it is possible, but it's just a bit harder for computers to surpass human level performance on natural perception tasks.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:38.080
And finally, all of these are problems where there are teams that have access to huge amounts of data.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:45.080
So for example, the best systems for all four of these applications have probably looked at far more data

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:49.080
of that application than any human could possibly look at.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:56.080
And so that's also made it maybe relatively easy for a computer to surpass human level performance.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:00.080
Now, the fact that there's so much data a computer could examine,

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:05.080
so they can better find statistical patterns than even a human might.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:12.080
Other than these problems, today there are speech recognition systems that can surpass human level performance.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:21.080
And there are also some computer vision, some image recognition tasks where computers have surpassed human level performance.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:28.080
But because humans are very good at these natural perception tasks, I think it was harder for computers to get there.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.080
And then there are some medical tasks.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:37.080
For example, reading ECGs or diagnosing skin cancer or certain narrow radiology tasks

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:44.080
where computers are getting really good and maybe surpassing a single human level performance.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:49.080
And I guess one of the exciting things about recent advances in deep learning is that even for these tasks,

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:53.080
we can now surpass human level performance in some cases.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:06:00.080
But it has been a bit harder because humans tend to be very good at these natural perception tasks.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:04.080
So surpassing human level performance is often not easy,

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:12.080
but given enough data, there have been lots of deep learning systems that have surpassed human level performance on a single supervised learning problem.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:15.080
So if it makes sense for an application you're working on,

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:21.080
I hope that maybe someday you manage to get your deep learning system to also surpass human level performance.
